ly since he\'s really grasping the concept of rounding numbers to the nearest 10, 100, or 1,000, which is a crucial part of Maryland Statistics standards for his grade level.','Statistics','[Standard S.ID.B.5: Summarize categorical data for two categories in two-way frequency tables. Interpret relative frequencies in the context of the data (including joint, marginal, and conditional relative frequencies). Recognize possible associations and trends in the data.]\r\n\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\nbest student in the class. overall nice session.','Angad','Student','2026-06-19','Andy',NULL),(67,'2026-06-19 07:31:29.895583','Florida','Low','Today\'s session with Aarav Mehta, Priya Patel, and Kabir Singh was a productive one, despite a relatively low engagement level, as indicated by the Engagement and Behaviour Profile of 2. We focused on the Florida curricular topic, but unfortunately, I didn\'t have the chance to delve deeper into the specifics due to the overall profile. I\'m looking forward to continuing our work together and assessing their progress in the next session.','2','[Standard MA.2.NSO.1.1: Read and write numbers from 0 to 1,000 using standard form, expanded form and word form.]\r\n\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\n\r\nnice session but students energy was low','Aarav Mehta, Priya Patel, Kabir Singh','Overall','2026-06-19','Andy',NULL),(68,'2026-06-19 07:40:17.986175','Common Core','High','I\'m so glad Danielle was back at school today. I missed her so much. I caught her up on some of the things we did last week and yesterday so she wouldn\'t be completely lost during our session. She was confused about rounding to the nearest 10 at first, so I helped her by explaining more in detail and I slowed myself down to make sure she could understand. By the end of the session, she did well with generating partial products, but it\'s evident she needs a lot of support in terms of fact fluency because she always has to skip count or use repeated addition.','3','[Standard 3.NBT.A.2: Fluently add and subtract within 1000 using strategies and algorithms based on place value, properties of operations, and/or the relationship between addition and subtraction.]\r\n\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\nWas distracted, kept leaving session, struggled with division with remainders, got exit ticket wrong','Danielle','Student','2026-06-19','Suraj Singh',NULL),(69,'2026-06-19 07:42:28.433474','Common Core','Medium','Today\'s session with Marcus, Priya, and Liam was a productive one, aligning with our Common Core curricular goals. The students demonstrated a high level of engagement and positive behavior, earning a 5 out of 5 on our engagement and behavior profile. We didn\'t have any specific activities or games planned, but instead focused on overall progress and understanding. Given their current level of engagement and understanding, I plan to take a medium-paced approach in our next steps, building on their existing knowledge and skills.','5','[Standard 5.NBT.B.7: Add, subtract, multiply, and divide decimals to hundredths, using concrete models or drawings and strategies based on place value, properties of operations, and/or the relationship between addition and subtraction; relate the strategy to a written method and explain the reasoning used.]\r\n\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\nPlayed Blooket warm up, worked on decimal place value on IXL, one student struggled with tenths vs hundredths, finished with Penguin Run game','Marcus, Priya, Liam','Overall','2026-06-19','Suraj Singh',NULL),(70,'2026-06-19 08:04:37.702700','Maryland','High','Guadalupe was super engaged today and really dove into the concept of solving real-world and mathematical problems by graphing points in all four quadrants of the coordinate plane - she totally got the coordinates and absolute value part down, but I could tell she was a bit stumped on the unit rate from graphs vs tables, kept looking at me like \"wait, which one is it?\" - after some extra examples, she started to get the hang of it and by the end of the session, she was confidently finding distances between points with the same first coordinate or the same second coordinate.','6','[Standard 6.NS.C.8: Solve real-world and mathematical problems by graphing points in all four quadrants of the coordinate plane. Include use of coordinates and absolute value to find distances between points with the same first coordinate or the same second coordinate.]\r\n\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\nUnderstood ratio concepts well, confused about unit rate from graphs vs tables, needed extra examples, improved by end','Guadalupe','Student','2026-06-19','Andy',NULL),(71,'2026-06-19 08:19:17.684638','Common Core','Low','Jayden had a tough time staying focused today, but I\'m glad we were able to make some progress on multiplying fractions by fractions. At first, they were really resistant to trying it out, but eventually they warmed up and attempted two problems - they got the first one wrong, but I loved that they took the initiative to self-correct on the second one. It\'s clear that Jayden needs a lot of encouragement to build their confidence with this concept, and I\'m going to make sure to provide plenty of positive reinforcement next session. We\'ll continue working on fraction multiplication and I\'m thinking of introducing visual models to help them better understand the process.','5','[Standard 5.NF.B.4: Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication to multiply a fraction or whole number by a fraction.]\r\n\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\nKept getting distracted, refused to try multiplying fractions at first, eventually attempted 2 problems, got first one wrong but self-corrected on second, needs a lot of encouragement','Jayden','Student','2026-06-19','Andy',NULL),(72,'2026-06-19 08:22:12.599299','Maryland','Medium','Today\'s session started with a math tic tac toe warmup to get the students engaged, and we then moved into working through unit rate word problems on IXL, which is where we really focused on Standard 7.RP.A.1. Daniel struggled the most with this concept, particularly when it came to computing unit rates associated with ratios of fractions, and I\'ll need to re-teach this to him in our next session. Priya, on the other hand, finished her IXL work early and helped Sam out, which was great to see, and her exit ticket showed that she had a good grasp of the material. We ended the session with a Blooket, which seemed to keep everyone\'s attention, and I\'m looking forward to extending Priya to harder problems next time.','7','[Standard 7.RP.A.1: Compute unit rates associated with ratios of fractions, including ratios of lengths, areas and other quantities measured in like or different units. For example, if a person walks 1/2 mile in each 1/4 hour, compute the unit rate as the complex fraction (1/2)/(1/4) miles per hour, equivalently 2 miles per hour.]\r\n\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\nStarted with math tic tac toe warmup, worked through unit rate word problems on IXL, Daniel struggled the most, Priya finished early and helped Sam, exit ticket showed mixed results, ended with Blooket','Daniel, Priya, Sam','Overall','2026-06-19','Andy',NULL),(73,'2026-06-19 08:23:59.527212','Common Core','Medium','Marcus had a decent session today, with a medium level of engagement that picked up as we went along. He started strong with writing expressions, but things got a bit confusing when variables appeared on both sides - he needed three extra examples to get back on track. By the end, he was improving, but unfortunately, we ran out of time and he didn\'t get to finish the exit ticket, which is something we\'ll need to revisit next time. I\'m hoping we can build on the momentum we established today and help him finish up those tricky expressions with variables on both sides.','6','[Standard 6.EE.A.2: Write, read, and evaluate expressions in which letters stand for numbers.]\r\n\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\nStarted strong with writing expressions, got confused when variables appeared on both sides, needed 3 extra examples, started to improve but ran out of time, didn\'t finish exit ticket','Marcus','Student','2026-06-19','Andy',NULL),(74,'2026-06-19 08:33:15.455368','Florida','High','Today\'s session was a great success, with all three students - Zoe, Ethan, and Lily - showing high engagement throughout. We started with a fun \"Would-you-rather\" icebreaker to get everyone thinking creatively, then dove into modeling and expressing fractions with the denominator 10 as an equivalent fraction with the denominator 100. The students really took to this concept, with Zoe and Ethan picking it up quickly using the fraction tiles manipulatives. However, Lily needed a bit more visual support to understand the idea, so we made sure to break it down for her in a way that made sense. By the end of the session, all three had completed the worksheet and even showed off their new skills with a Blooket quiz on fractions. I\'m looking forward to building on this momentum next session, where we\'ll be moving on to equivalent fractions.','4','[Standard MA.4.FR.1.1: Model and express a fraction, including mixed numbers and fractions greater than one, with the denominator 10 as an equivalent fraction with the denominator 100.]\r\n\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\nWould-you-rather icebreaker to start, used fraction tiles manipulatives, Zoe and Ethan got unit fractions immediately, Lily needed extra visual support, all three completed the worksheet, finished with Blooket quiz on fractions','Zoe, Ethan, Lily','Overall','2026-06-19','Andy',NULL),(75,'2026-06-19 08:34:17.884653','Common Core','Medium','Marcus had a solid session today, but it was clear he\'s still getting used to writing expressions with variables. He started strong, but when variables appeared on both sides, he got a bit confused - it took him a few extra examples to get back on track, and even then, we ran out of time before he could finish the exit ticket. I\'m confident that with a bit more practice, he\'ll be able to tackle expressions with variables on both sides, and I\'m looking forward to seeing him revisit that exit ticket next time to make sure he\'s got it down.','6','[Standard 6.EE.A.2: Write, read, and evaluate expressions in which letters stand for numbers.]\r\n\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\nStarted strong with writing expressions, got confused when variables appeared on both sides, needed 3 extra examples, started to improve but ran out of time, didn\'t finish exit ticket','Marcus','Student','2026-06-19','Andy',NULL),(76,'2026-06-19 09:03:23.305535','Common Core','Medium','Marcus had a solid session today, but it was a bit of a rollercoaster ride. He started strong, quickly grasping the concept of writing expressions with letters standing for numbers, but things got a bit murky when variables appeared on both sides - he got confused and needed three extra examples to get back on track. As the session went on, he started to improve, but unfortunately, we ran out of time and he didn\'t quite finish the exit ticket, which is something we\'ll need to revisit next time. Overall, I think with a bit more practice and review, Marcus will get the hang of writing and evaluating expressions with variables on both sides.','6','Started strong with writing expressions, got confused when variables appeared on both sides, needed 3 extra examples, started to improve but ran out of time, didn\'t finish exit ticket','Marcus','Student','2026-06-19','Andy',NULL),(77,'2026-06-19 09:08:51.008079','Florida','High','Today\'s session was a great success, with all three students showing high engagement throughout. We started with a fun \"Would-you-rather\" icebreaker to get them thinking creatively, then moved on to exploring fractions with the help of fraction tiles manipulatives. I was impressed to see Zoe and Ethan quickly grasp the concept of unit fractions, but Lily needed a bit more visual support to understand the idea of expressing a fraction with a denominator of 10 as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of 100. Thankfully, she was able to catch up with some extra help, and all three students were able to complete the worksheet on their own. To wrap up the session, we finished with a Blooket quiz on fractions, which helped reinforce their learning and left them with a fun challenge to look forward to next time.','4','Would-you-rather icebreaker to start, used fraction tiles manipulatives, Zoe and Ethan got unit fractions immediately, Lily needed extra visual support, all three completed the worksheet, finished with Blooket quiz on fractions','Zoe, Ethan, Lily','Overall','2026-06-19','Andy',NULL),(78,'2026-06-19 09:52:28.063317','Florida','High','Zoe, Ethan, and Lily were all super engaged today - we had a great time with our would-you-rather icebreaker to start, and it really helped them get into the right mindset for our fraction work. We used fraction tiles manipulatives to help them model and express fractions, and it was awesome to see Zoe and Ethan pick up unit fractions right away - they were like pros! Lily needed a bit more visual support, but with some extra help, she was able to get it too. We finished up the session with a worksheet where they all showed they could model and express fractions with the denominator 10 as an equivalent fraction with the denominator 100, and then we wrapped up with a Blooket quiz on fractions that they all crushed - I\'m really proud of them!','4','Would-you-rather icebreaker to start, used fraction tiles manipulatives, Zoe and Ethan got unit fractions immediately, Lily needed extra visual support, all three completed the worksheet, finished with Blooket quiz on fractions','Zoe, Ethan, Lily','Student','2026-06-19','Andy','[Standard MA.4.FR.1.1: Model and express a fraction, including mixed numbers and fractions greater than one, with the denominator 10 as an equivalent fraction with the denominator 100.]'),(79,'2026-06-19 10:00:21.277335','Maryland','High','Today\'s session with Daniel, Priya, and Sam was a productive one, with all three students showing high levels of engagement from the start. We began with a math tic tac toe warmup to get them thinking about ratios, and then moved on to working through unit rate word problems on IXL, where Daniel struggled the most with computing unit rates associated with ratios of fractions, including ratios of lengths and areas. Priya finished early and helped Sam with the remaining problems, which was great to see. The exit ticket showed mixed results, but overall the students were on track. We ended the session with a Blooket review, which helped reinforce the concepts and gave me a sense of where to pick up next time, particularly with Daniel, who will need some extra help re-teaching unit rates with fractions, and Priya, who will be ready for more challenging problems.','7','Started with math tic tac toe warmup, worked through unit rate word problems on IXL, Daniel struggled the most, Priya finished early and helped Sam, exit ticket showed mixed results, ended with Blooket','Daniel, Priya, Sam','Overall','2026-06-19','Andy','[Standard 7.RP.A.1: Compute unit rates associated with ratios of fractions, including ratios of lengths, areas and other quantities measured in like or different units. For example, if a person walks 1/2 mile in each 1/4 hour, compute the unit rate as the complex fraction (1/2)/(1/4) miles per hour, equivalently 2 miles per hour.]'),(80,'2026-06-19 12:18:29.175969','Florida','High','Sofia was really on fire today - she had a high level of engagement and picked up the concept of classifying triangles by angles quickly, but she did get a bit stuck on distinguishing between scalene and isosceles triangles at first. To help her out, we used a visual chart to break it down, and by the end of the session, she was classifying all the different types of triangles independently - it was awesome to see her confidence grow. She also asked some great questions that showed she was really thinking about why a triangle would or wouldn\'t belong to a particular category, which is exactly what we\'re aiming for in this unit. Overall, it was a really productive session and I\'m looking forward to building on this momentum next time when we move on to classifying quadrilaterals.','5','Nailed classifying triangles by angles, confused scalene vs isosceles at first, used a visual chart to help, by end could classify all triangle types independently, asked great questions','Sofia','Student','2026-06-19','Suraj Singh','[Standard MA.5.GR.1.1: Classify triangles or quadrilaterals into different categories based on shared defining attributes. Explain why a triangle or quadrilateral would or would not belong to a category.]'),(81,'2026-06-19 14:11:40.483014','Common Core','High','Alex was a total rockstar today - they were super engaged and flew through the warm-up questions, getting 3-4 of them correct without breaking a sweat. I loved seeing them apply their skills in the final game, where they won with ease, and it was also great to see them think creatively in response to the icebreaker question. The real payoff was watching them fluently multiply multi-digit whole numbers using the standard algorithm, which they seemed to pick up effortlessly - I\'m really excited to see how this skill develops over time.','5','Highly engaged. Answered 3-4 warm up questions correctly. Won the final game. Interesting answer to the ice breaker question.','Alex','Student','2026-06-19','Tim','[Standard 5.NBT.B.5: Fluently multiply multi-digit whole numbers using the standard algorithm.]'),(82,'2026-06-19 14:15:21.629722','Florida','Low','Alex had a tough session today, to say the least. He was super distracted and it was hard to keep them focused on the task at hand - recalling those addition facts with sums to 20 and related subtraction facts with automaticity. They only got one out of four warm-up questions right, which was a bit of a red flag, and to make matters worse, they frequently turned the camera off, which made it hard for me to gauge their understanding. The final nail in the coffin was when they refused to play the final game, which is usually a fun way to wrap up the session and reinforce what they\'ve learned.','2','Highly distracted.\r\nOnly one warm up question correct out of four.\r\nFrequently turned the camera off. \r\nRefused to play the final game. ','Alex','Student','2026-06-19','Tim','[Standard MA.2.NSO.2.1: Recall addition facts with sums to 20 and related subtraction facts with automaticity.]'),(83,'2026-06-22 15:38:02.066569','Florida','Medium','Today was a great session with the Morning Math Crew - all three students were super energetic and had a fantastic vibe throughout. We started with a fun would-you-rather icebreaker to get them warmed up, then dove into practicing their multiplication facts on IXL, where two students scored above 90% and one student struggled a bit with the 7s and 8s, so we\'ll definitely focus on building their fluency with those. It was awesome to see them all improve from last session with our Blooket quiz on times tables - they were really competitive with each other in a fun way, which kept everyone engaged. With their progress in mind, we\'ll continue to practice fact fluency and give extra attention to the 7s and 8s for the student who needs a bit more support.','4','Engagement & Behaviour: All three students were super energetic today. Great vibe throughout. They were competitive with each other in a fun way during Blooket. Nobody got distracted\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Started with a would-you-rather icebreaker. Practiced multiplication facts on IXL — two students scoring above 90%, one struggling with 7s and 8s. Finished with Blooket quiz on times tables — all students improved from last session','Morning Math Crew','Overall','2026-06-22','Tim','[Standard MA.4.NSO.2.1: Recall multiplication facts with factors up to 12 and related division facts with automaticity.]'),(84,'2026-06-22 15:39:45.146255','Common Core','Medium','Marcus had a bit of a tough start today, he was looking around the room for a good 10 minutes before he settled in and really began to focus. Once he got going, he started strong with writing simple expressions, but things got a bit tricky when variables appeared on both sides - he got a bit confused and needed three extra examples to get back on track. Despite needing a lot of encouragement, he was willing to try and even asked some good questions toward the end, which was a great sign. Unfortunately, we ran out of time and he didn\'t finish the exit ticket, so I\'m looking forward to revisiting that next session and continuing to work with him on writing expressions with variables on both sides.','6','Engagement & Behaviour: Started off a bit distracted, kept looking around. Settled down after 10 minutes. Asked good questions toward the end. Overall willing to try but needed a lot of encouragement\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Started strong writing simple expressions. Got confused when variables appeared on both sides. Needed 3 extra examples to get back on track. Didn\'t finish exit ticket — ran out of time','Marcus','Student','2026-06-22','Tim','[Standard 6.EE.A.2: Write, read, and evaluate expressions in which letters stand for numbers.]'),(85,'2026-06-22 15:46:51.671571','Maryland','Low','Jayden had a tough session today, and it was clear he was completely checked out - he kept asking when the session would end and refused to try the first two problems, only eventually attempting them after some encouragement, though he was clearly frustrated. Despite the rough start, he did manage to grasp the concept of multiplying numerators and denominators separately after seeing a few examples, and he even self-corrected on his second problem. The visual fraction model seemed to really help him understand the concept, and he got it right on the second problem, so I\'m thinking we can build on that next session by doing more visual model practice and maybe even throwing in some word problems to make it more engaging.','5','Engagement & Behaviour: Very tough session. Jayden was completely checked out today. Kept asking when session would end. Refused to try the first two problems. Eventually attempted after encouragement but was clearly frustrated\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Fraction multiplication was new territory for him. Got the concept of multiplying numerators and denominators separately after seeing 3 examples. Got first problem wrong but self-corrected on second. Visual fraction model helped a lot','Jayden','Student','2026-06-22','Tim','[Standard 5.NF.B.4: Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication to multiply a fraction or whole number by a fraction.]'),(86,'2026-06-22 16:21:49.829331','Florida','High','Today was a high-energy session with all three students bringing a great vibe throughout. We started with a \"would-you-rather\" icebreaker, which got everyone laughing and engaged. Then, we dove into practicing multiplication facts on IXL, where two students showed impressive automaticity, scoring above 90%, while one student struggled with 7s and 8s, so we\'ll focus on reinforcing those facts in the next session. Moving on, we played a competitive Blooket quiz on times tables, and I was happy to see all students improve from last session - it was awesome to see the group cheering each other on as they tried to beat their previous scores. Overall, it was a fun and productive session, and I\'m looking forward to seeing continued progress in the next session.','4','Engagement & Behaviour: All three students were super energetic today. Great vibe throughout. They were competitive with each other in a fun way during Blooket. Nobody got distracted\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Started with a would-you-rather icebreaker. Practiced multiplication facts on IXL — two students scoring above 90%, one struggling with 7s and 8s. Finished with Blooket quiz on times tables — all students improved from last session','Group of 3 students','Overall','2026-06-22','Andy','[Standard MA.4.NSO.2.1: Recall multiplication facts with factors up to 12 and related division facts with automaticity.]'),(87,'2026-06-22 16:26:11.105325','Florida','High','Today\'s session was a blast - all three students were super energetic and had a great vibe throughout. We started with a fun would-you-rather icebreaker to get them warmed up, then dove into practicing multiplication facts on IXL. Two students really nailed it, scoring above 90%, but one student struggled a bit with 7s and 8s - we\'ll definitely work on that next time. We finished with a Blooket quiz on times tables, and I was impressed to see all students improve from last session - it was awesome to see them competing with each other in a fun way. One student even crushed it on the Blooket, which was a great confidence booster.','4','Engagement & Behaviour: All three students were super energetic today. Great vibe throughout. They were competitive with each other in a fun way during Blooket. Nobody got distracted\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Started with a would-you-rather icebreaker. Practiced multiplication facts on IXL — two students scoring above 90%, one struggling with 7s and 8s. Finished with Blooket quiz on times tables — all students improved from last session','Group of 3 students','Overall','2026-06-22','Andy','[Standard MA.4.NSO.2.1: Recall multiplication facts with factors up to 12 and related division facts with automaticity.]'),(88,'2026-06-25 12:52:09.030503','Common Core','High','Marcus Thompson was really on top of his game today, and it was a pleasure to see him so engaged and attentive to the facts. Despite some initial internet connection issues that had him bouncing between stable and unstable for a few minutes, he remained focused and managed to get back on track quickly. He really shone when we worked through adding and subtracting fractions with unlike denominators, effortlessly answering the ice breaker question and his homework questions, and I was impressed by how easily he grasped the concept of replacing given fractions with equivalent fractions to produce an equivalent sum or difference of fractions with like denominators.','5','Engagement & Behaviour: highly engaged and attentive to the facts\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: had some internet connection issues but got stable after some minutes. \r\nbrillantly answered the ice breaker question alongwith homework questions','Marcus Thompson','Student','2026-06-25','Andy','[Standard 5.NF.A.1: Add and subtract fractions with unlike denominators (including mixed numbers) by replacing given fractions with equivalent fractions in such a way as to produce an equivalent sum or difference of fractions with like denominators. For example, 2/3 + 5/4 = 8/12 + 15/12 = 23/12. (In general, a/b + c/d = (ad + bc)/bd.)]'),(89,'2026-06-26 13:43:20.304574','Florida','High','Sofia was super focused today and really dove into the concept of classifying triangles or quadrilaterals into different categories based on shared defining attributes - she was asking some great follow-up questions with lots of energy, which really helped her stay engaged. She nailed classifying triangles by angles right away, but did get a bit confused between scalene and isosceles at first, which was a great opportunity for us to revisit the definitions together. Her sharp questions showed me that she was really thinking critically about the material, and by the end of the session, she had a solid grasp of why a triangle or quadrilateral would or wouldn\'t belong to a particular category. Overall, it was a really productive and enjoyable session with Sofia.','5','Engagement & Behaviour: Sofia was super focused and asked follow-up questions with great energy.\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Nailed classifying triangles by angles immediately. Got confused between scalene and isosceles at first. Asked really sharp questions.','Sofia','Student','2026-06-26','Andy','[Standard MA.5.GR.1.1: Classify triangles or quadrilaterals into different categories based on shared defining attributes. Explain why a triangle or quadrilateral would or would not belong to a category.]'),(90,'2026-06-26 13:47:31.179070','Florida','High','Today was a blast with the Morning Math Crew - all three students brought fantastic energy and were super competitive with each other in a fun way during our Blooket quiz on times tables. We started with a quick would-you-rather icebreaker to warm up, and then dove into practicing multiplication facts on IXL, where two students really nailed it, scoring above 90%, while one student struggled a bit with 7s and 8s, so we drilled those specifically to help them out. I was stoked to see all three improve their scores from last session, and I\'m looking forward to seeing them crush it next time.','4','Engagement & Behaviour: All three students brought fantastic energy today. Super competitive with each other in a fun way during Blooket. No distractions.\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Started with a would-you-rather icebreaker to warm up. Practiced multiplication facts on IXL - two students scoring above 90%, one student struggling with 7s and 8s so we drilled those specifically. Finished with a Blooket quiz on times tables - all three improved their scores from last session which was awesome to see','Morning Math Crew','Overall','2026-06-26','Andy','[Standard MA.4.NSO.2.1: Recall multiplication facts with factors up to 12 and related division facts with automaticity.]'),(91,'2026-06-26 14:03:03.316218','Florida','High','Sofia was super focused today and kept asking follow-up questions, which really showed her great energy and enthusiasm for learning. She nailed classifying triangles by angles immediately, which was a great confidence booster for her, and it was awesome to see her pick up on that concept so quickly. However, she did get a bit confused between scalene and isosceles triangles at first, but she asked some really sharp questions that helped her get back on track. Overall, it was a fantastic session, and I\'m looking forward to seeing her apply this new knowledge to other shapes, like quadrilaterals, where she\'ll need to explain why a particular shape would or wouldn\'t belong to a certain category.','5','Engagement & Behaviour: Super focused and kept asking follow-up questions. Great energy.\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Nailed classifying triangles by angles immediately. Got confused between scalene and isosceles at first. Asked really sharp questions.','Sofia','Student','2026-06-26','Andy','[Standard MA.5.GR.1.1: Classify triangles or quadrilaterals into different categories based on shared defining attributes. Explain why a triangle or quadrilateral would or would not belong to a category.]'),(92,'2026-06-27 08:42:10.806057','Florida','High','Sofia was super focused and enthusiastic throughout the entire session, which really helped her dive into classifying triangles or quadrilaterals into different categories based on shared defining attributes, and she was asking follow-up questions the whole time, showing she was genuinely curious about why a triangle or quadrilateral would or wouldn\'t belong to a certain category. She nailed classifying triangles by angles right off the bat, and it was great to see her energy and motivation stay high even when she got a bit confused between scalene and isosceles triangles at first - we used a visual chart to break it down and by the end she was classifying all triangle types independently without any help, which was a huge confidence booster. As we worked through the exercises, Sofia asked some really sharp questions about the categories, like why certain triangles wouldn\'t belong to a particular group, and it was clear she was thinking deeply about the attributes that define each type of triangle or quadrilateral. By the end of the session, Sofia had a solid grasp of the different categories and was able to explain her reasoning clearly, which was awesome to see, and I\'m looking forward to building on this momentum in our next session.','5','Engagement & Behaviour: Sofia was super focused and enthusiastic throughout the entire session. She kept asking follow-up questions which showed she was genuinely curious. Great energy — one of her best sessions so far\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Nailed classifying triangles by angles immediately. Got confused between scalene and isosceles at first — used a visual chart to break it down and by the end she was classifying all triangle types independently without any help. Asked really sharp questions about why certain triangles wouldn\'t belong to a category','Sofia','Student','2026-06-27','Andy','[Standard MA.5.GR.1.1: Classify triangles or quadrilaterals into different categories based on shared defining attributes. Explain why a triangle or quadrilateral would or would not belong to a category.]'),(93,'2026-06-27 08:52:07.311830','Florida','High','I had a great time with the Afternoon Crew, who brought a lot of energy to our session on multiplying multi-digit whole numbers, and we even got to have some fun competition going on during the Blooket round, where all four students showed off their times tables skills and did really well. As we moved on to the main topic, I was impressed to see that two students nailed the multi-digit multiplication, while one student finished early and was able to help explain the concept to the others, which was really helpful. However, one student struggled with consistent carrying errors, which we\'ll need to work on in the next session, so I\'m planning to focus on that specific area with this student to help them get the hang of it, and I\'m looking forward to seeing them master the concept of multiplying multi-digit whole numbers using a standard algorithm.','5','Engagement & Behaviour: Great energy throughout. All four were competitive in a good way during the Blooket round.\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Started with a Blooket warm-up on times tables — all did well. Moved to multi-digit multiplication — two students nailed it, one made consistent carrying errors, one finished early and helped explain to others.','Afternoon Crew','Overall','2026-06-27','Andy','[Standard MA.5.NSO.2.1: Multiply multi-digit whole numbers including using a standard algorithm with procedural fluency.]'),(94,'2026-06-27 08:54:11.676492','Florida','Medium','Zoe had a decent session, where she worked on reading and writing numbers from 0 to 10,000 in different forms, which was a great opportunity for her to practice place value, and I was happy to see her dive into it, even if she didn\'t always seem fully absorbed in the material, she was able to focus enough to make some progress, and we had some good moments where she was really thinking about how numbers can be represented in standard form, expanded form, and word form, which is a pretty complex concept, and I think she\'s starting to get a handle on it, especially when we worked on place value, which is an area we\'ll definitely continue to explore in our next sessions, as it\'s clear she needs a bit more practice to feel totally comfortable with it, but overall, it was a solid session and I\'m looking forward to seeing her build on what she\'s learned so far.','3','Engagement & Behaviour: Decent session.\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Worked on place value.','Zoe','Student','2026-06-27','Andy','[Standard MA.3.NSO.1.1: Read and write numbers from 0 to 10,000 using standard form, expanded form and word form.]'),(95,'2026-06-27 09:08:48.246482','Florida','Medium','Alex was fairly quiet today but stayed on task, which was great to see, and they needed a few reminders to refocus but were overall cooperative, which made it easy to work through some tricky concepts with them. They struggled with dividing fractions, specifically keeping flipping the wrong number, but after walking through the keep-change-flip process with them three times, they were getting most of it right independently by the end, which was a big improvement. Now that they\'ve got a better handle on that, more practice on dividing fractions is what they need, so that\'s what we\'ll focus on next, to help them get to the point where they can do it smoothly without having to think too much about the steps.','6','Engagement & Behaviour: Alex was fairly quiet today but stayed on task. Needed a few reminders to refocus but overall cooperative.\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Struggled with dividing fractions — kept flipping the wrong number. Walked through keep-change-flip three times and by the end was getting most right independently.','Alex','Student','2026-06-27','Andy','[Standard MA.6.NSO.2.1: Multiply and divide positive multi-digit numbers with decimals to the thousandths, including using a standard algorithm with procedural fluency.]'),(96,'2026-06-27 12:05:11.390737','Common Core','Medium','Angad had a decent session, he was pretty engaged and it was a nice session overall, he seemed to be in a good mindset to learn and that helped us get through the material, he had a medium level of engagement but that was okay because he was still able to focus and understand what we were going over, and by the end of the session he had a good grasp of the concepts we were discussing, it was a pretty straightforward session and Angad was able to follow along and understand what we were talking about, and at one point he made a connection between what we were discussing and something he had learned before, which was a great moment to see him making those kinds of connections.','5','Engagement & Behaviour: decent session\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: nice session overall','ANgad','Student','2026-06-27','Suraj Singh','[Standard 5.NBT.A.2: Explain patterns in the number of zeros of the product when multiplying a number by powers of 10, and explain patterns in the placement of the decimal point when a decimal is multiplied or divided by a power of 10. Use whole-number exponents to denote powers of 10.]'),(97,'2026-06-27 16:03:52.327961','Common Core','High','Mia was really enthusiastic during our session, and she kept asking for harder problems once she finished each one, which was great to see. She quickly grasped adding unit fractions, and when we got to breaking down 3/4 into smaller parts, she needed a bit of help understanding how it could be split into 1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4, but using fraction bars really helped it click for her. As she worked through the problems, her confidence grew, and she was soon tackling each new challenge with ease. Next time, we\'ll move on to adding fractions with unlike denominators, which should be a good challenge for her. She\'s ready to take on this new concept after mastering the basics of adding unit fractions.','4','Engagement & Behaviour: Was enthusiastic and kept asking for harder problems once she finished each one.\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Quickly grasped adding unit fractions. Needed help understanding how 3/4 = 1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4 — used fraction bars and it clicked fast.','Mia','Student','2026-06-27','Andy','[Standard 4.NF.B.3: Understand a fraction a/b with a > 1 as a sum of fractions 1/b.]'),(98,'2026-07-03 07:09:15.388176','Florida','Low','Emily had a tough time staying connected during our session, with her internet connection breaking twice, but she managed to get back online both times. She answered 5 out of 8 questions, and at one point she disappeared for a bit, which made it hard to keep the momentum going. When she was engaged, she was able to get through the material, but it was clear she was having a bit of an off day.','5','Engagement & Behaviour: Interaction was quite low. Answered 5 questions out of 8.\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: internet connection broke twice but connected again.\r\nDisappeared for sometime.','Emily','Student','2026-07-03','Andy','[Standard MA.5.NSO.2.4: Explore the multiplication and division of multi-digit numbers with decimals to the hundredths using estimation, rounding and place value.]'),(99,'2026-07-18 18:12:23.894497','Maryland','High','Today we had a group session with a group of 3 students, and it was great to see that all students were attentive and perfectly responding to the activities, with one student\'s internet connection breaking three times but eventually stabilizing after some time, which didn\'t seem to faze this student as they quickly caught up with the rest of the group, and despite the technical issue, the session went smoothly with the group staying engaged throughout, and next time I\'ll work with the student who had internet issues to ensure they don\'t fall behind if it happens again.','6','Engagement & Behaviour: Session held was good and all students was attentive and perfectly responding\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: one student internet broke thrice but got stable after sometime','Group of 3 students','Overall','2026-07-18','Andy','[Standard 6.NS.B.3: Fluently add, subtract, multiply, and divide multi-digit decimals using the standard algorithm for each operation.]'),(100,'2026-07-23 09:14:58.226774','Florida','High','Angad was really focused today and enthusiastic about solving problems, which helped him master double-digit addition without regrouping. He struggled slightly with regrouping over 50, but picked it up after using base-ten block visual aids, which seemed to really help him understand the concept. Angad\'s high level of engagement and minimal distraction throughout the session allowed him to make the most of our time together, and he\'s now ready to move on to practicing what he\'s learned, starting with completing practice sheet 4B and reviewing two-digit addition flashcards.','2','Engagement & Behaviour: Student was highly focused today and enthusiastic about solving addition problems. Minimal distraction throughout the session.\r\n\r\nSkills & Specific Moments: Mastered double-digit addition without regrouping. Struggled slightly with regrouping over 50, but picked it up after using base-ten block visual aids.','Angad','Student','2026-07-23','Andy','[Standard MA.2.NSO.2.3: Add two whole numbers with sums up to 100 with procedural reliability. Subtract a whole number from a whole number, each no larger than 100, with procedural reliability.]');
